# KAMPYN — Performance Rules

## 1. Purpose

Performance is a system property, not merely a code-level concern.

These rules define how KAMPYN should reason about:

- algorithmic complexity
- memory usage
- database performance
- API performance
- network usage
- frontend rendering
- search
- caching
- concurrency
- background processing
- scalability
- performance measurement

The objective is not to make every operation maximally fast.

The objective is to ensure that KAMPYN remains:

- responsive
- resource-efficient
- predictable under load
- scalable
- operationally affordable
- resistant to performance regressions

Do not optimize blindly.

Do not sacrifice correctness or maintainability for theoretical performance.

---

# 2. Performance Hierarchy

When making performance decisions, consider the problem in this order:

```text
Correctness
    ↓
Algorithm / Data Structure
    ↓
I/O and Network
    ↓
Database
    ↓
Caching
    ↓
Concurrency
    ↓
Runtime Optimization
    ↓
Micro-optimization
```

A poor algorithm should not be "fixed" with caching.

A poor database query should not be "fixed" with frontend optimization.

A network-heavy workflow should not be optimized by shaving CPU instructions.

Fix the highest-impact bottleneck first.

---

# 3. Measure Before Optimizing

Do not assume what the bottleneck is.

When practical, establish:

- current behavior
- expected workload
- input size
- latency
- throughput
- memory usage
- CPU usage
- database query behavior
- network cost
- concurrency
- error rate

Use actual measurements when the optimization is performance-sensitive.

Avoid statements such as:

```text id="f8k2n0"
"This is faster."
"This should scale."
"This is more efficient."
```

without evidence when the claim materially affects architecture or performance.

---

# 4. Complexity Standards

Prefer algorithms with appropriate asymptotic complexity.

Typical preferred complexity:

```text id="e3r6a1"
O(1)
O(log n)
O(n)
O(n log n)
```

Avoid unnecessary:

```text id="v9d3m4"
O(n²)
O(n³)
O(2ⁿ)
```

when a practical alternative exists.

Before accepting an algorithm for large or unbounded input, determine:

- time complexity
- space complexity
- worst-case behavior
- expected input size
- expected frequency
- concurrency
- memory pressure

---

# 5. O(n²) Policy

O(n²) is not universally forbidden.

It requires justification when the workload makes it relevant.

An O(n²) solution may be acceptable when:

- the input is demonstrably small
- the input is tightly bounded
- the operation is infrequent
- the simpler implementation is materially easier to maintain
- a better asymptotic solution would introduce disproportionate complexity

Avoid O(n²) when the operation handles:

- large datasets
- user-generated collections
- database result sets
- search indexes
- high-frequency requests
- background workloads
- batch processing
- large imports/exports

When replacing O(n²), consider:

- hash maps
- sets
- indexing
- sorting
- binary search
- merge algorithms
- grouping
- precomputation
- database-side operations

---

# 6. Data Structures

Choose data structures based on access patterns.

Consider:

```text id="0a8q7s"
lookup
insertion
deletion
ordering
iteration
deduplication
memory
concurrency
```

Examples:

Use a `Set` when membership and deduplication are primary concerns.

Use a `Map` when key-based lookup is required.

Use arrays when ordered iteration and sequential access dominate.

Use indexes when persistent lookup is frequent.

Do not repeatedly scan a collection when an appropriate indexed or hashed representation is available.

---

# 7. Repeated Work

Avoid performing the same expensive operation repeatedly.

Watch for:

- repeated parsing
- repeated serialization
- repeated computation
- repeated database queries
- repeated network requests
- repeated searches
- repeated transformations
- repeated allocations

Possible solutions include:

- memoization
- caching
- precomputation
- batching
- indexing
- materialized results
- request coalescing

Do not cache or memoize automatically.

First establish that repeated work is actually expensive or frequent enough to justify the added complexity.

---

# 8. Memory

Performance includes memory behavior.

Avoid unnecessary:

- large object duplication
- full dataset materialization
- unbounded arrays
- unbounded maps
- repeated serialization buffers
- retained references
- copies of large payloads

Prefer:

- streaming
- pagination
- chunking
- bounded buffers
- incremental processing
- iterators
- backpressure

when working with large datasets.

Do not load an entire large dataset into memory when the operation can safely be performed incrementally.

---

# 9. Large Data Processing

Large datasets must be processed with explicit resource limits.

Consider:

```text id="e0x6e1"
input size
memory limit
CPU cost
I/O throughput
parallelism
chunk size
backpressure
failure recovery
retry behavior
```

For large files or datasets:

Prefer:

```text id="1r0u8e"
streaming
chunking
partitioning
incremental processing
bounded concurrency
```

Avoid:

```text id="d4j9r2"
load everything into memory
```

unless the dataset is demonstrably bounded and the memory requirement is acceptable.

---

# 10. Database Performance

Database performance must be considered before adding or modifying queries.

Inspect:

- query shape
- indexes
- cardinality
- selectivity
- joins
- sorting
- filtering
- pagination
- aggregation
- transaction scope
- lock behavior
- query frequency

Avoid:

- N+1 queries
- unbounded result sets
- unnecessary round trips
- repeated identical queries
- accidental full-table scans
- unnecessary joins
- excessive indexes
- fetching unused columns

Prefer:

- appropriate indexes
- batching
- pagination
- projections
- set-based operations
- prepared queries
- appropriate query plans

---

# 11. N+1 Queries

N+1 behavior is prohibited when an efficient alternative is practical.

Bad pattern:

```text id="3d1j7h"
Fetch 100 orders
    ↓
Fetch customer for order 1
Fetch customer for order 2
Fetch customer for order 3
...
```

Prefer:

```text id="h6p4v1"
Fetch orders
    ↓
Batch required customer data
    ↓
Associate results in memory
```

or an appropriate database join/query.

Always inspect query behavior when loops interact with persistence.

---

# 12. Database Indexing

Indexes should be based on actual access patterns.

Before adding an index, consider:

- query frequency
- selectivity
- cardinality
- write frequency
- storage cost
- index maintenance
- compound index ordering
- query patterns

Do not add an index merely because a field "might be searched."

Do not create excessive indexes.

Every important high-frequency query should have an intentional indexing strategy.

---

# 13. Pagination

Never expose unbounded collections through public APIs or user-facing queries.

For large datasets, use appropriate pagination.

Consider:

```text id="m4k9p7"
offset pagination
cursor pagination
keyset pagination
```

based on workload and consistency requirements.

For large or frequently changing datasets, prefer cursor/keyset approaches where appropriate.

Pagination must define:

- page size
- maximum page size
- ordering
- stable cursor semantics
- filtering behavior

Never allow clients to request arbitrarily large pages.

---

# 14. API Performance

API performance includes:

```text id="j5p3w7"
server processing
database time
external calls
serialization
network transfer
client processing
```

Avoid:

- unnecessary fields
- unnecessary nested objects
- repeated requests
- sequential requests that can safely be parallelized
- unbounded responses
- redundant external calls

Prefer:

- appropriate pagination
- field selection where justified
- batching
- caching
- request coalescing
- parallel independent operations

Do not optimize response payloads prematurely when the payload is already small and insignificant.

---

# 15. Network Calls

Network calls are expensive compared with local computation.

Minimize unnecessary:

- HTTP requests
- database round trips
- service-to-service calls
- cloud API calls
- search requests

When multiple independent operations can safely execute concurrently, consider parallel execution.

However, concurrency must respect:

- provider rate limits
- database capacity
- memory
- CPU
- downstream service limits

Do not blindly execute every operation concurrently.

---

# 16. External Services

Every external dependency introduces:

- latency
- failure
- rate limits
- retry behavior
- operational cost

Before calling an external service:

- determine whether the call is necessary
- determine whether results can be cached
- determine timeout behavior
- determine retry behavior
- determine idempotency
- determine rate limits

Do not place external calls inside database transactions unless explicitly required and safe.

---

# 17. Caching

Caching is an optimization and consistency mechanism.

Every cache must have a deliberate:

```text id="q8m2d1"
key
TTL
invalidation strategy
ownership
consistency expectation
failure behavior
```

Do not introduce a cache without understanding invalidation.

Avoid caching:

- highly volatile data without a consistency strategy
- data with low reuse
- data cheaper to compute than to cache
- sensitive data without appropriate isolation

Prefer caching when:

- reads significantly exceed writes
- computation is expensive
- external calls are expensive
- data is reused frequently
- latency requirements justify it

---

# 18. Cache Keys

Cache keys must uniquely identify the data and relevant scope.

For tenant-aware data, cache keys must preserve tenant isolation.

Do not use ambiguous keys such as:

```text id="q5j7v8"
user:123
```

when the resource also depends on:

```text id="0f3g5j"
tenant
locale
permissions
version
filters
```

where those dimensions affect the result.

Cache-key design must be treated as part of correctness.

---

# 19. Cache Invalidation

Cache invalidation must be intentional.

When authoritative data changes, determine whether:

- the cache must be invalidated
- the cache can tolerate stale data
- the cache should be refreshed
- the cache should use versioned keys
- eventual consistency is acceptable

Never assume:

```text id="q6g2j4"
"we can invalidate it later"
```

is an acceptable architecture.

If stale data can cause incorrect business behavior, caching must not bypass the required consistency guarantees.

---

# 20. OpenSearch Performance

Search workloads should be designed around the search engine rather than treating OpenSearch as a generic database.

Consider:

- index mappings
- analyzers
- field types
- shard strategy
- replica strategy
- query complexity
- result size
- pagination
- filtering
- aggregations
- indexing throughput

Avoid:

- unnecessarily expensive queries
- retrieving huge result sets
- indexing fields that are never searched
- excessive aggregations
- deeply paginated searches where an appropriate cursor/search-after strategy exists

Search indexes should remain purpose-built for their workload.

---

# 21. Search Result Limits

Never return unbounded search results.

Define appropriate:

- maximum result size
- pagination strategy
- sorting
- filtering
- timeout behavior

For large search workloads, prefer efficient cursor/search-after approaches where supported and appropriate.

Do not make search requests retrieve more data than the UI or consumer requires.

---

# 22. Frontend Performance

Frontend performance should be considered across:

```text id="1x5r2m"
network
server rendering
client rendering
JavaScript execution
state updates
DOM size
assets
images
data fetching
```

Avoid:

- unnecessary client-side rendering
- unnecessary JavaScript
- excessive state
- redundant API requests
- unnecessary re-renders
- large synchronous computations
- unnecessarily large component trees

Prefer server-side capabilities when they reduce unnecessary client work.

Follow the project's frontend architecture rather than introducing ad-hoc optimization patterns.

---

# 23. React Rendering

Do not optimize every component prematurely.

First identify actual rendering problems.

Watch for:

- unnecessary state
- unstable references
- expensive calculations during render
- unnecessary context updates
- large lists
- repeated data transformations

Use memoization only when it provides a meaningful benefit.

Avoid:

```text id="8k4c3x"
useMemo everywhere
useCallback everywhere
React.memo everywhere
```

without evidence.

Memoization has complexity and maintenance costs.

---

# 24. Large Frontend Lists

Large lists should not render thousands of unnecessary DOM nodes.

For large datasets, consider:

- pagination
- virtualization
- incremental loading
- server-side filtering
- server-side sorting
- debounced search

Choose based on actual interaction requirements.

Do not load an entire large dataset into the browser merely because the API makes it possible.

---

# 25. Client State and Server State

Server state should not be duplicated unnecessarily across client state systems.

Use the project's established state architecture.

In particular:

```text id="x2j6n8"
Server State
    ↓
TanStack Query
```

and:

```text id="y4f1c8"
Client/UI State
    ↓
Zustand
```

Avoid copying server responses into global client state unless there is a documented reason.

Duplicated state creates synchronization and invalidation costs.

---

# 26. Bundle and Asset Performance

Avoid unnecessary frontend payload.

Consider:

- code splitting
- lazy loading
- tree shaking
- dependency size
- image size
- font loading
- third-party scripts

Do not add large dependencies for small functionality.

Prefer existing project capabilities when they satisfy the requirement.

Do not optimize bundle size by introducing excessive abstraction or fragmented loading that harms usability.

---

# 27. Debouncing and Throttling

Use debouncing or throttling when user interactions can trigger excessive work.

Common examples:

- search input
- autocomplete
- resize events
- scroll handlers
- repeated API requests

Choose based on the behavior required.

Do not use arbitrary delays to hide architectural or performance problems.

---

# 28. Concurrency

Concurrency should be bounded.

Before parallelizing work, consider:

- CPU availability
- memory
- downstream capacity
- database connection limits
- API rate limits
- queue capacity
- contention

Prefer bounded concurrency over unlimited parallelism.

Bad:

```text id="z7r5d4"
spawn one worker per item
```

for an unbounded dataset.

Prefer:

```text id="4m3p8s"
bounded worker pool
```

or an equivalent controlled mechanism.

---

# 29. Go Concurrency

For Go services:

- Every goroutine must have a clear lifecycle.
- Every goroutine should have a termination path.
- Respect context cancellation.
- Avoid goroutine leaks.
- Avoid unbounded goroutine creation.
- Protect shared mutable state.
- Consider channel ownership carefully.
- Avoid concurrency when sequential execution is already sufficient.

Do not introduce goroutines merely because they can improve theoretical throughput.

---

# 30. Background Processing

Background jobs should use controlled resource consumption.

Consider:

```text id="7c1h2v"
queue depth
worker count
batch size
retry count
retry delay
timeout
memory
CPU
downstream limits
```

A background worker should not consume unlimited resources simply because work is available.

Use backpressure where appropriate.

---

# 31. Retry Behavior

Retries can amplify outages.

Every retry policy should consider:

- maximum attempts
- timeout
- backoff
- jitter
- idempotency
- error classification
- downstream capacity

Do not retry:

- permanent validation failures
- authorization failures
- malformed requests
- known non-retryable business errors

without a deliberate reason.

Use exponential backoff where appropriate.

Avoid immediate tight retry loops.

---

# 32. Timeouts

Every external or potentially long-running operation should have an appropriate timeout.

Avoid:

```text id="6z1t9x"
infinite waits
unbounded retries
requests without cancellation
```

Timeouts should reflect the expected operation rather than using one arbitrary global value for everything.

Timeouts should propagate through the appropriate execution context.

---

# 33. Resource Limits

Every potentially unbounded system should have explicit limits.

Consider limits for:

- request body size
- response size
- file size
- page size
- queue depth
- concurrency
- retries
- cache size
- search result size
- upload size
- processing duration

Unbounded behavior is a scalability risk.

---

# 34. Serialization

Serialization can become expensive for large payloads.

Avoid:

- serializing data that is never consumed
- repeatedly converting between formats
- unnecessary deep cloning
- unnecessarily verbose payloads
- repeated JSON encoding/decoding in hot paths

Prefer streaming or incremental processing for sufficiently large payloads.

Do not optimize serialization before establishing that it is a meaningful bottleneck.

---

# 35. Memory Allocation

Avoid unnecessary allocations in hot paths.

Pay attention to:

- large temporary collections
- repeated object creation
- repeated string concatenation
- unnecessary copies
- duplicate buffers
- serialization intermediates

For Go:

- avoid premature pooling
- use profiling before introducing `sync.Pool`
- understand escape behavior before micro-optimizing allocations

Correctness and clarity remain more important than minor allocation reductions.

---

# 36. Hot Paths

A hot path is code executed:

- frequently
- for many users
- for many records
- inside large loops
- inside high-throughput services

Hot paths deserve additional scrutiny.

When modifying one:

1. Identify expected frequency.
2. Identify dominant cost.
3. Measure where practical.
4. Avoid unnecessary allocations.
5. Avoid repeated I/O.
6. Review algorithmic complexity.
7. Review concurrency.
8. Verify the improvement.

Do not micro-optimize cold paths without evidence.

---

# 37. Performance Regression Prevention

Performance-sensitive functionality should have regression protection where practical.

Potential mechanisms include:

- benchmarks
- load tests
- query-plan checks
- response-time thresholds
- memory checks
- bundle-size checks
- search benchmarks

Do not introduce arbitrary thresholds without understanding normal variance.

Performance tests should be stable enough to provide useful signals.

---

# 38. Scalability

Every major design should consider growth.

At minimum, think about:

```text id="n3y7q1"
10× current workload
100× current workload
large tenants
large datasets
high concurrency
failure conditions
database growth
search growth
```

This does not mean designing for infinite scale.

It means avoiding architectural decisions that obviously fail at foreseeable scale.

---

# 39. Horizontal Scaling

Services intended to scale horizontally should avoid unnecessary local state.

Be cautious with:

- in-memory sessions
- local-only caches
- local filesystem state
- process-local locks
- process-local queues
- mutable global state

If multiple instances may run simultaneously, state that must be shared should use an appropriate shared system.

---

# 40. Vertical vs. Horizontal Scaling

Do not assume horizontal scaling automatically solves performance problems.

Before scaling infrastructure, determine whether the bottleneck is:

- CPU
- memory
- database
- network
- storage
- search
- external provider
- inefficient algorithm
- inefficient query

Scale the actual bottleneck where practical.

---

# 41. Database Connection Management

Database connections are finite resources.

Avoid:

- creating unnecessary clients
- opening connections per request
- leaking connections
- unbounded connection pools
- excessive concurrent queries

Use the project's established connection-management strategy.

Connection pool sizing should consider:

```text id="0p4y6z"
application instances
database capacity
query latency
concurrency
workload
```

More connections do not automatically mean more performance.

---

# 42. Batch Operations

Batch operations when repeated individual operations create unnecessary overhead.

Prefer:

```text id="c4v9j8"
100 records
    ↓
1 appropriate batch operation
```

over:

```text id="m7k2w1"
100 records
    ↓
100 individual network/database calls
```

when the operation semantics permit batching.

Do not create giant unbounded batches.

Choose bounded batch sizes based on resource limits and workload.

---

# 43. Streaming

Use streaming when data is:

- large
- sequential
- incrementally processable
- expensive to fully materialize

Streaming is particularly appropriate for:

- large files
- exports
- imports
- logs
- data pipelines
- large API responses

Do not use streaming merely because it sounds more scalable.

Ensure:

- backpressure
- cancellation
- error handling
- resource cleanup

are properly handled.

---

# 44. File and Object Storage

Large files should generally avoid unnecessary local memory materialization.

Prefer:

```text id="j3c7m9"
stream
→ process
→ upload/store
```

rather than:

```text id="q8m4x2"
download entire file
→ load into memory
→ process
→ upload
```

when the workflow permits streaming.

Consider:

- file size
- transfer time
- retries
- checksum/integrity
- memory
- temporary storage
- parallelism

---

# 45. Performance and Correctness

Performance optimization must never silently change business behavior.

Do not:

- drop validation
- weaken authorization
- reduce consistency guarantees
- remove required transactions
- bypass domain rules
- return stale data where freshness is required
- skip error handling

merely to improve performance.

If a performance optimization changes consistency or correctness guarantees, the trade-off must be explicit and architecturally approved.

---

# 46. Performance Review Checklist

Before completing a performance-sensitive change:

### Algorithm

- [ ] Time complexity considered.
- [ ] Space complexity considered.
- [ ] Avoidable O(n²) behavior removed or justified.
- [ ] Data structures match access patterns.

### Database

- [ ] Query behavior inspected.
- [ ] N+1 behavior ruled out.
- [ ] Indexing considered.
- [ ] Result size bounded.
- [ ] Unnecessary round trips avoided.

### Network

- [ ] Unnecessary calls removed.
- [ ] Independent work is safely parallelized where useful.
- [ ] Timeouts exist.
- [ ] Retry behavior is intentional.

### Memory

- [ ] Large data is not unnecessarily materialized.
- [ ] Unbounded collections are avoided.
- [ ] Copies and allocations are reasonable.

### Caching

- [ ] Cache necessity is established.
- [ ] Keys are correct.
- [ ] TTL is defined.
- [ ] Invalidation is understood.
- [ ] Staleness is acceptable.

### Concurrency

- [ ] Concurrency is bounded.
- [ ] Cancellation is handled.
- [ ] Resource limits are respected.
- [ ] No leaks are introduced.

### Frontend

- [ ] Unnecessary rendering avoided.
- [ ] Large lists are handled appropriately.
- [ ] Redundant network requests avoided.
- [ ] Server/client responsibilities remain appropriate.

### Verification

- [ ] Relevant benchmarks or measurements were considered.
- [ ] Performance-sensitive tests pass.
- [ ] No correctness regression was introduced.

---

# 47. Final Principle

KAMPYN performance should come from good engineering decisions, not layers of hacks.

Prefer:

```text id="4m8p2r"
better algorithm
      ↓
better data structure
      ↓
better query
      ↓
less I/O
      ↓
appropriate caching
      ↓
bounded concurrency
      ↓
measurement
      ↓
optimization
```

over:

```text id="1n6v9c"
premature optimization
      ↓
more caching
      ↓
more concurrency
      ↓
more infrastructure
      ↓
more complexity
```

The goal is not to make every operation theoretically optimal.

The goal is to make KAMPYN **fast enough, scalable enough, predictable under load, and simple enough to maintain**.

> **Measure before optimizing.  
> Fix algorithms before micro-optimizing.  
> Reduce I/O before shaving CPU cycles.  
> Bound resources before scaling them.  
> Cache deliberately, not reflexively.  
> Parallelize intentionally, not blindly.  
> Never trade correctness for speed.**